  :adr/body "# ADR-2608131001: Two scheduled jobs that could not fail

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-08-13
**Follows**: ADR-2608124400 (shallow was not retired everywhere) -- findings 1 and 2 of its
launchd audit, deliberately left unfixed there
**Touches**: ADR-2607173100 (murakumo-main SSoT alias) / ADR-2607172800 (SSE is the public route)

## Which two jobs, and how they were identified

ADR-2608124400 described both by behaviour rather than by name. Both were identified
from their logs and their code, and the identification is unambiguous.

**Job 1 -- `com.gftd.playtest-coscientist`**, running `~/.gftd/run-playtest-coscientist.cljs`
weekly (plist `StartCalendarInterval` = Weekday 1, Hour 5, i.e. Monday 05:00). Its log
`~/.gftd/playtest-coscientist.log` is 1,465 lines. Line 1461 is the last line of a run
that really happened (`[run-playtest] done; server stopped`, preceded by a Clojure stack
trace through `isekai.capture$capture_game`). Lines 1462-1465 are four byte-identical
copies of

    [standing-playtest] no changes — no candidate beat baseline this run

with nothing between them, and `grep -c` finds exactly four occurrences of that string in
the whole file. Last write 2026-08-10 05:00. That is the ADR's \"four consecutive weekly
no-ops\" exactly.

**Job 2 -- `com.gftd.shinshi-kaizen-tick`**, running `~/.gftd/run-shinshi-kaizen-tick.cljs`
daily at 09:13. Its log is 21 lines for 21 expected runs: twenty
`[shinshi-kaizen-tick] ERROR: LLM HTTP 400` and one `... HTTP 502`, in that order, ending
2026-08-12 09:13. Never a success line.

## What job 1 was actually doing

`network-isekai` moved org. It is now `orgs/network-awai/network-isekai` in
`manifest/west.yml` (`remote: network-awai`, 855 commits, `is-shallow: false`). The script
hardcoded

    (def wt \".../orgs/gftdcojp/network-isekai-playtest\")

and passed it as `:cwd` to every `spawnSync`. **`spawnSync` with a nonexistent `cwd` does
not throw** -- it returns an object with `status` = nil and `.error` set to an ENOENT
spawn error. The script's `run` helper discarded the return value entirely. So all seven
commands -- fetch, checkout, reset, clean, npm ci, build, the playtest itself -- silently
did nothing, and the final

    (let [dirty (out \"git\" [\"status\" \"--porcelain\"] {})]
      (if (empty? dirty) (println \"...no candidate beat baseline...\")))

read an empty string from a command that never ran and printed the benign line. Exit 0.

**Reproduced before changing anything.** Running the pre-fix script verbatim, unmodified,
from the backup copy:

    [standing-playtest] no changes — no candidate beat baseline this run
    ORIGINAL EXIT CODE: 0

That single line is the whole run. It is not a false report -- an empty diff of nothing is
empty -- which is precisely why it survived four weeks of `launchctl` showing green.

There was a **second** staleness the path fix alone would not have caught: this checkout's
git remote is named `network-awai`, not `origin`, so `git fetch origin main` would have
failed too.

## What job 2 was actually doing -- not the cause on the record

ADR-2608124400 recorded the cause as a hardcoded model id against a legacy hostname. That
diagnosis is **wrong about the 400s**, and the correction matters more than the fix.

The `murakumo-main` alias does resolve to a different endpoint than the one baked in --
measured today, `GET https://api.murakumo.cloud/infer/models/murakumo-main` returns
`alias-for: qwen3.6-35b-a3b`, `endpoint: https://infer.murakumo.cloud/v1/chat/completions`,
while the script hardcoded `https://qwen-gad.gftd.ai/v1/chat/completions`. But that
hostname was never dead: a small hand-built request to it returned **HTTP 200** with a
normal completion. Replaying the script's own prompt against **both** endpoints returned
the same 400, with the same body:

    {\"error\":{\"code\":400,\"message\":\"Expected 'messages' to be an array\",\"type\":\"invalid_request_error\"}}

The request body was built with a `#js {...}` literal. **`#js` is shallow.** `:messages`
stayed a ClojureScript vector containing a ClojureScript map, and `JSON.stringify`
serialised the PersistentVector's *internal fields*:

    {\"model\":\"m\",\"messages\":{\"G\":null,\"I\":1,\"shift\":5,\"root\":{...}},...}

against the correct `clj->js` output of

    {\"model\":\"m\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":\"hi\"}],\"max_tokens\":8}

So every request since the job was created was malformed, independently of endpoint and
model. The server said so in plain words each time, and the script threw the body away:
`(throw (js/Error. (str \"LLM HTTP \" (.-status r))))`. Twenty identical uninformative
lines were the result of discarding the one sentence that would have ended this in a
minute.

A third defect was hiding behind the second. Once the body was well-formed, the request
still failed -- **HTTP 524 at ~128 s**. The public endpoints sit behind Cloudflare, whose
edge gives up if the origin has not *started* responding within roughly 100 s, and a
non-streaming completion of this ~38,000-character prompt buffers until generation
finishes. The same request with `stream: true` returned **HTTP 200, first byte at 17.9 s,
complete at 47.0 s**. ADR-2607172800 already names SSE as the public route; the job simply
was not using it. The original 120 s client-side `AbortSignal.timeout` was under the
measured completion time as well.

## What changed

### `~/.gftd/run-playtest-coscientist.cljs`

The path correction is the small half. The durable half is that a wrong path can no longer
be quiet.

- **Every child process goes through one checked helper.** A spawn `.error`, a **nil**
  `status`, or any non-zero `status` prints `FAIL(<named-cause>)` and exits non-zero.
  There is no unchecked `spawnSync` left in the file. `kagi get MURAKUMO_CRITIC_TOKEN` is
  the single deliberate exception -- the loop documents a degraded offline-heuristic critic
  when the token is absent -- and it still reports.
- **The repo path is resolved from `manifest/west.yml` by project NAME**, not hardcoded to
  an org. A rename is what broke this job; the name is what survives a rename. If the
  project ever leaves the manifest, that is itself a named failure
  (`project-not-in-manifest`) rather than a guess.
- **The remote name is resolved**, not assumed to be `origin`.
- **The dedicated worktree is created on demand**, and **never with `--depth`** -- linked
  worktrees share `.git/shallow` with the parent repository, which is the re-shallowing
  vector ADR-2608124400 identified.
- `PLAYTEST_REPO_PATH` and `PLAYTEST_MANIFEST` override the resolution for operators; they
  are also what made the guard testable against the unmodified script.

### `~/.gftd/run-shinshi-kaizen-tick.cljs`

- `clj->js` instead of `#js`, so the body converts recursively.
- **Model and endpoint resolve per ADR-2607173100**: env override (`GFTD_LLM_URL` /
  `GFTD_LLM_MODEL`), then the `murakumo-main` KV alias, then an endpoint-only fallback.
  **No concrete model id is baked anywhere** -- on the fallback path the model name is read
  back from that endpoint's own `/v1/models`, which is what \"bake only an endpoint\" means
  operationally.
- **Streaming**, with an SSE reader that accumulates `choices[0].delta.content`.
- The client timeout moved from 120 s to 600 s, and HTTP failures now carry the server's
  own message instead of just a status number.

Its existing `run!` helper was checked and is **not** vulnerable to job 1's trap: measured
in nbb, `(zero? nil)` returns false rather than throwing, so `(when-not (zero? status)
(throw ...))` already hard-fails on a nil status.

## Firing the guard

A guard nobody has watched fire is not a guard. All four failure branches were fired
against the **unmodified, installed** script, and the two that matter are the two the old
code silently swallowed.

    # the exact path the original hardcoded -- the original's failure condition
    $ PLAYTEST_REPO_PATH=.../orgs/gftdcojp/network-isekai-playtest nbb run-playtest-coscientist.cljs
    [standing-playtest] FAIL(git-remote): git [\"remote\"] could not run:
      spawnSync git ENOENT (cwd=\".../orgs/gftdcojp/network-isekai-playtest\")
    EXIT: 1

    # directory exists, is not a git repo -> the non-zero branch
    $ PLAYTEST_REPO_PATH=$(mktemp -d) nbb run-playtest-coscientist.cljs
    [standing-playtest] FAIL(git-remote): git [\"remote\"] exited 128 (cwd=\"/var/folders/...\")
      stderr=fatal: not a git repository (or any of the parent directories): .git
    EXIT: 1

    # project renamed out of the manifest -- the next org move, simulated
    $ PLAYTEST_MANIFEST=/tmp/west-renamed.yml nbb run-playtest-coscientist.cljs
    [standing-playtest] FAIL(project-not-in-manifest): no `- name: network-isekai` entry in
      /tmp/west-renamed.yml — the project was renamed or removed; this run cannot guess a path.
    EXIT: 1

    $ PLAYTEST_MANIFEST=/tmp/nope.yml nbb run-playtest-coscientist.cljs
    [standing-playtest] FAIL(manifest-missing): /tmp/nope.yml does not exist
    EXIT: 1

**Deliberately avoided a fake demonstration.** Breaking something the script never reads
would also have produced a clean-looking pass. Both negative cases above break the value
the script's *first* child process consumes, and there is no `existsSync` pre-check on the
repo path -- it was removed on purpose, so that a missing checkout is caught by exactly the
spawn guard that was absent before, rather than by a separate check that would leave the
spawn guard untested.

**The positive direction found a real breakage rather than a synthetic one.** With no
overrides, the script resolved `orgs/network-awai/network-isekai` from the manifest,
resolved remote `network-awai`, fetched, created the worktree
`orgs/network-awai/network-isekai-playtest`, hard-synced it to `network-awai/main`
(fcca735), and ran `npm ci` to completion -- all returning 0, which is the evidence that
the guard does not false-positive. Then:

    Error building classpath. Manifest file not found for kotoba-lang/org-threejs in
      coordinate #:local{:root \".../orgs/kotoba-lang/org-threejs\"}
    [standing-playtest] FAIL(build): node [... \"npm\" \"run\" \"build\"] exited 1
    EXIT: 1

`orgs/kotoba-lang/org-threejs` was a **half-populated west checkout**: it had a `.git`, a
`node_modules/` and one source file, but no `HEAD` and no `deps.edn`. This is a real
breakage of the job's build, present for an unknown time, that the old script would have
swallowed along with everything else. It was repaired: the two untracked files blocking
`west update` (`.gitignore`, `src/org_threejs/vrm.cljs`) were confirmed **byte-identical**
to the pinned revision 606854d by `git hash-object` before removal, and
`west update --fetch smart org-threejs` then landed the pin cleanly.

## What was not exercised, and why

- **Job 1's build/playtest/push tail was not run to completion.** Three attempts were made
  after repairing `org-threejs`; all three were refused by the shared resource governor,
  which is correct behaviour -- concurrent sessions held the `build` lock
  (`resource-guard: build is already running (pid=..., repo=/private/tmp/wtroot/...)`),
  and the guard named it (`FAIL(build) ... exited 2`). Everything up to and including
  `npm ci` ran for real and returned 0. The playtest itself launches Playwright browsers
  and can push a branch, so it was not forced.
- **Job 2 was not run end to end, on purpose.** It commits, pushes and **auto-merges to
  `main` of a live repo** (`gh api repos/jk-luxury/club-shinshi-app/merges`), and that is
  the owner's decision, not this session's. What *was* exercised is the LLM path in
  isolation, with the script's own `resolve-llm` / `read-sse!` / `call-llm` extracted
  verbatim from the installed file and driven with the real 38,149-character prompt built
  from the real repo state: **HTTP 200 via the murakumo-main alias, a 20,860-character
  reply, parseable `===FILE:` blocks.** Nothing was written, committed, pushed or merged.
  The whole file was additionally compile-checked by loading it with the trailing
  `(-main)` stripped.
- One consequence worth stating plainly: a week in which another session holds the build
  lock will now show a **red** job. That is the intended trade. `resource-guard` exit 2
  means \"someone else is building\", not \"the build is broken\", and a wait-and-retry would
  be a change to when a scheduled job runs -- the owner's call, not made here.

## Finding 3 -- `~/.gftd`: the blocker named in ADR-2608124400 is already gone

The brief for this work assumed the migration was blocked because the plists carry
absolute paths. **Measured today, that blocker no longer exists**, and the count in
ADR-2608124400 is off.

**The count.** 25 plists in `~/Library/LaunchAgents` match the string `.gftd`, but 13 of
those match only because their *Label* contains it (`ai.gftd.monitor`, `com.gftd.*`).
**12 plists actually reference the directory `~/.gftd`**, and all 12 are loaded in
`launchctl list`. Of those 12, only **five execute code stored there**:

| plist | what it runs from `~/.gftd` |
| --- | --- |
| `ai.gftd.murakumo` | `run-murakumo-daemon.sh` |
| `com.gftd.hayari-mirror` | `hayari-mirror/bin/tick.cljs` (inside a git clone of `cloud-itonami/hayari`) |
| `com.gftd.playtest-coscientist` | `run-playtest-coscientist.cljs` |
| `com.gftd.shinshi-kaizen-tick` | `run-shinshi-kaizen-tick.cljs` |
| `com.gftdcojp.itonami.state-snapshot` | `bin/itonami-state-snapshot.sh` |

The other seven only write logs there, or read one credential path
(`com.gftd.fleet-ci-tip-tick` sets `FLEET_CI_SIGNER_PEM` / `FLEET_CI_GH_TOKEN_DIR`;
`com.gftd.murakumo-actions-runner` cats a token file). **Nothing that merely logs there
needs to move**, which shrinks the problem by more than half before any plan is written.

**The relocation is unnecessary, and a session a few hours earlier already did the right
thing instead.** `~/.gftd` is under version control **in place**, with the git dir
deliberately outside the work tree at `~/.gftd-vcs.git`:

    git --git-dir=$HOME/.gftd-vcs.git --work-tree=$HOME/.gftd <cmd>

commit `1e58e22` \"Put ~/.gftd's scheduled scripts under version control, in place\".
Nothing moved, no plist changed, no file was renamed. A plain `git init` there would have
changed git repository *discovery* for every subdirectory -- including
`~/.gftd/fleet-ci-cache`, which holds thousands of deliberately git-less extracted repo
trees that a launchd job refreshes every five minutes, and whose git-less-ness some gates
rely on to refuse a false pass. `.gitignore` is deny-by-default (`*` plus an explicit
allowlist), so a new credential dropped in that flat namespace is excluded unless someone
opts it in. Seven scripts are tracked. **The two fixes in this ADR were committed there**
as `f29be83`, which is the first time either of these scheduled scripts has had a
reviewable diff.

**What is still open, and what an owner would need to approve.** The honest residual is
not relocation:

1. **`~/.gftd-vcs.git` has no remote.** The history that protects these scripts lives on
   the same disk as the scripts. A remote (a small private repo, or a bare repo on B2 via
   the existing tooling) is one `git remote add` plus one push, and needs only a decision
   about where. This is the single highest-value follow-up.
2. **`scripts/audit-gftd-scripts.cljs` is still wired to nothing.** It works -- run today
   it reports 3 violations, all `.sh` files, one of them (`run-murakumo-daemon.sh`) live
   under `ai.gftd.murakumo`. ADR-2608125000 landed a home for exactly this class of
   detector (`manifest/orgs-detectors.edn`, surfaced by the SessionStart hook
   `.claude/hooks/session-start-orgs-detectors.cljs`, both on `main`), but only two
   detectors are registered and this is not one of them -- because registering a detector
   that reports a standing red is itself against that ADR's rule. The prerequisite is
   therefore deciding the three `.sh` files' fate (migrate to nbb, or record an explicit
   exemption), **then** registering it. A fleet-ci gate cannot substitute: fleet ships a
   repo tree to a node, and `~/.gftd` is not in any tree -- the same structural reason
   `root-permit-index` could not pose its question.
3. **The remaining unversioned executable surface is the four nested git clones**
   (`club-shinshi-app-kaizen`, `hayari-mirror`, `hayari-tick`, `hayari-data`). These are
   working copies of repos that already have upstreams; they are correctly excluded by the
   allowlist and need no migration, only the awareness that `com.gftd.hayari-mirror`
   executes from one of them, so its code review happens in `cloud-itonami/hayari`, not
   here.

No relocation was performed, and none is recommended.

## Consequences

- Both jobs can now fail. Job 1 will go red on a stale path, a renamed project, a missing
  manifest, a broken sibling dependency, or a held build lock -- each with a named cause on
  one line. Job 2 will report the server's own error text.
- The class of defect is worth naming beyond these two files: **a scheduled job whose
  success line is computed from a command that never ran**. The log line was true; the
  inference from it was not. Any standing job that reports an outcome derived from a child
  process it did not check has the same shape.
- `#js` is shallow, and this is the second time in this workspace that a nested
  ClojureScript collection inside a `#js` literal produced a wrong wire payload that a
  server rejected with a precise message nobody read. Prefer `clj->js` for anything that
  reaches `JSON.stringify`, and never discard an HTTP error body."}]
